  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Mon Jun 15 2026\n\nThe NBM is still insisting that Wednesday and Thursday will be \nvery hot with many locations reaching 100 degrees for the first \ntime this season. Models show continuing northwesterly mid and \nupper flow helps advect drier and hotter air east into the New \nMexico plains and Permian Basin in the form of a dryline. Remain \nskeptical that we will see that much heating behind the dryline \ngiven the relatively cool temperatures aloft. It is hard to see \nhow some of these cooler temps will not mix to the surface and \nmodify the air some, especially if we mix out to 700mb. \nRegardless, we are talking a degree or too so it will be very hot \nnonetheless.\n\nThe northwesterly flow brings the dryline across on Wednesday and\nit also pushes a cold front south into the CWA on Thursday. Again\nthere is uncertainty in the temperature forecast as highs will \ngreatly depend on the progression of the front before peak \nheating. It will likely reach to at least the northern counties of\nthe basin allowing for some cooling and areas farther south, \nespecially south of I-20, having little effect. The front pushes \nsouth overnight and Friday will again have below normal \ntemperatures. Rain chances increase as moisture advects in behind \nthe front with the greatest uncertainty being in coverage. Given \nthe poor rainfall of this current front the next one does not have\nmuch more lift to expect high rain chances. The NBM indicates the\nbest rain chances are Friday afternoon in the higher elevations \nof the Big Bend as well as to the east in the lower Trans Pecos \nwith orographic lift and the eastern basin which is closest to a \nweak upper disturbance.\n\nAfterward high pressure builds pressure builds in over NM/TX \ngreatly reducing rain chances the remainder of the forecast \nperiod. Temps increase back to near normal for this time of year \nwith highs reaching the 90s to low 100s. Relatively modest 500mb \nheights near 590dam and easterly mid level flow should prevent \nscorching temperatures from returning. \n\nHennig",
